  "id": "Compound::DB00280 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Disopyramide, a drug known for its effects on muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, appears to interact with Chrm3 through a shared pathway involving G-protein coupled receptor activity. The evidence suggests that Disopyramide binds to Chrm2, which then participates in G-protein coupled receptor activity, ultimately leading to Chrm3 being part of this activity. This indicates a potential mechanism where Disopyramide influences Chrm3 via a common intermediary, Chrm2, through the activation of G-protein coupled receptors. The overlapping participation of Chrm2 in different receptor activities—such as acetylcholine and neurotransmitter receptors—suggests a broader role in receptor signaling, which may explain the drug's effects on Chrm3. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible dependency on muscarinic receptor activity, reinforcing the notion that Disopyramide may modulate Chrm3 through a complex network of receptor interactions.",
